1
00:00:09,481 --> 00:00:15,753
All right, for the last video in this
module that we're going to talk about for

2
00:00:15,753 --> 00:00:21,192
the network layer are really
about tools for troubleshooting.

3
00:00:21,192 --> 00:00:24,913
So troubleshooting is one of
the most kind of important and

4
00:00:24,913 --> 00:00:29,680
sometimes the most challenging
parts of network administration.

5
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:35,233
And so there's some common
tools that are the go-tos for

6
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:39,248
at least kind of the first level checks.

7
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:44,570
And so we'll cover what those are and
roughly how they work.

8
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,463
So the first one is ping.

9
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:53,833
So this is really helpful
to do a quick check

10
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:58,913
if a server is up and if it's reachable.

11
00:00:58,913 --> 00:01:02,444
It also includes information
such as round trip time so

12
00:01:02,444 --> 00:01:05,938
you can identify if there's
some latency issues.

13
00:01:05,938 --> 00:01:12,780
And so shown here is an example of
performing a ping to colorado.edu and

14
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:17,226
you can see that you're
getting responses and

15
00:01:17,226 --> 00:01:21,570
you can get how long
the round trip time took.

16
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:28,246
So how ping works is it's
actually just a kind of

17
00:01:28,246 --> 00:01:36,391
specific type of packet that's
within the payload of an IP packet.

18
00:01:36,391 --> 00:01:41,378
So it uses a protocol called ICMP and

19
00:01:41,378 --> 00:01:45,700
it performs the type of echo.

20
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:51,807
And so shown here is basically your
1.1.1.1, you want to perform ping.

21
00:01:51,807 --> 00:01:57,410
What actually is happening is
it forms a message of this form,

22
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:01,935
an Ethernet, IP and
ICMP headers, sends the ICMP

23
00:02:01,935 --> 00:02:06,894
request destined to the IP
address 2.2.2.2 and

24
00:02:06,894 --> 00:02:10,800
then that will respond with an ICMP reply.

25
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:18,410
And you could see in the IPV4 header,
we saw this before, the protocol field.

26
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:22,280
So that would be protocol = 1 for ICMP.

27
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:28,395
And then within the ICMP header which
would be the payload of the IP packet,

28
00:02:28,395 --> 00:02:34,040
it would just say type which would
be 0 for a request, 1 for a reply.

29
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,020
So that's ping and ICMP.

30
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:45,634
The next tool that's kind of
useful is called traceroute and

31
00:02:45,634 --> 00:02:49,288
this gives you a path to a destination.

32
00:02:49,288 --> 00:02:54,190
So this is helpful if you think,
all right, there's either latency issues

33
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:58,260
going through the network or
reachability issues.

34
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:01,810
And you want to say, right,
let me run a traceroute,

35
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:04,842
there is reachability or latency issues.

36
00:03:04,842 --> 00:03:07,868
I want to see the path of routers
that it's going through and

37
00:03:07,868 --> 00:03:09,985
maybe there's something unexpected.

38
00:03:09,985 --> 00:03:15,458
So for example, I see in a message go,
I'm in Colorado and

39
00:03:15,458 --> 00:03:19,594
I'm just connecting to
a server locally but

40
00:03:19,594 --> 00:03:25,950
my messages are going all the way
to Europe or something like that.

41
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:30,580
And so that's some sort of indication
of a problem and I can identify that by

42
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:35,150
looking at the routers, this trace
of routers that it's going through.

43
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:40,359
So how this is implemented is if you

44
00:03:40,359 --> 00:03:45,197
recall the TTL field, the time to live.

45
00:03:45,197 --> 00:03:53,186
So what you do is, let's say we have this
kind of topology in between the client.

46
00:03:53,186 --> 00:03:57,132
This is my machine that I
want to do a traceroute from and

47
00:03:57,132 --> 00:04:01,955
then in between that to the server
that I want to do a traceroute to,

48
00:04:01,955 --> 00:04:06,700
and then there's the sequence
of routers in between.

49
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:09,425
So how do I discover each?

50
00:04:09,425 --> 00:04:14,755
Is I first send a message with
the destination to 2.2.2.2 and

51
00:04:14,755 --> 00:04:17,582
I set the time to live field = 1.

52
00:04:17,582 --> 00:04:22,991
So when I send that out, when it gets
to the first router, it's going to

53
00:04:22,991 --> 00:04:28,680
decrement the time to live field and
then it's going to drop the packet.

54
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:35,500
But many routers will actually say,
not just drop the packet silently but

55
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:43,452
they'll actually respond with an ICMP
message that says the type, time exceeded.

56
00:04:43,452 --> 00:04:46,972
So it actually indicates
there's a problem here and

57
00:04:46,972 --> 00:04:51,690
this is a good troubleshooting,
that's the reason why they do it.

58
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:56,238
But to support traceroute
also it can be used for that.

59
00:04:56,238 --> 00:05:00,045
So it'll set its source address,

60
00:05:00,045 --> 00:05:05,559
send it back to whoever was
the original source and

61
00:05:05,559 --> 00:05:08,568
set the ICMP as type 11.

62
00:05:08,568 --> 00:05:13,644
So when that gets back,
now this traceroute utility knew,

63
00:05:13,644 --> 00:05:18,904
all right,
at one hop I have 5.5.5.5 is the router.

64
00:05:18,904 --> 00:05:23,938
Then I'll send another packet
as destined to 2.2.2.2,

65
00:05:23,938 --> 00:05:28,300
set the time to live field now 2,
I want to go two hops.

66
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:33,560
And so it goes to the second hop and
it gets the time exceeded.

67
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:38,750
Now, some of the kind of responses,
not all routers support this and

68
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:45,050
so that's where you might see just kind of
blank lines or the starred lines in that.

69
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:49,276
All right, so that's traceroute.

70
00:05:49,276 --> 00:05:55,998
Now, the last one is called iperf or
iperf3 for the third version of it.

71
00:05:55,998 --> 00:06:02,360
Now, iperf allows you to determine the max
throughput between a client and a server.

72
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:07,225
And so this is useful when you start
seeing some performance issues and

73
00:06:07,225 --> 00:06:11,062
you want to start narrowing
it down to what's going on.

74
00:06:11,062 --> 00:06:12,695
Is it something with the application?

75
00:06:12,695 --> 00:06:15,580
Is it something inside of the network?

76
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:21,782
And so you can run an iperf test
by running a client version,

77
00:06:21,782 --> 00:06:28,119
that's what the dash C stands for
and then a server version.

78
00:06:28,119 --> 00:06:34,021
So what this will do is it'll open up,
it'll listen for connections, and

79
00:06:34,021 --> 00:06:41,160
then the iperf program will start sending
traffic to the destination to 2.2.2.2.

80
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:44,558
And it'll keep increasing and
it'll try to find,

81
00:06:44,558 --> 00:06:48,270
what is the maximum amount
of traffic that it can send?

82
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:51,792
And then it'll start reporting
at each interval and

83
00:06:51,792 --> 00:06:55,362
then it'll kind of stabilize
once it's found that.

84
00:06:55,362 --> 00:07:00,318
And you can see in this case it's saying
the rate that it's able to send at

85
00:07:00,318 --> 00:07:02,361
is 94 megabits per second.

86
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:10,244
All right, so those are just
some tools that are useful for

87
00:07:10,244 --> 00:07:14,288
troubleshooting at the network layer.

88
00:07:14,288 --> 00:07:20,193
And with that, we kind of wrap up
the videos for the network layer module.

89
00:07:20,193 --> 00:07:21,129
Thanks.